the prime minister said there are signs  of a second wave of the virus in europe  but is this the case if you look at the  countries that are most affected in the  world you'll see it's places like brazil  the united states india and south africa  the number of new cases are continuing  to rise  and these countries haven't reached  their first peak yet  in europe many countries are beginning  to relax lockdown  and travel restrictions because the rate  of infection is decreasing  however in luxembourg rates of infection  have increased by 117.6  points in the last fortnight it's a  small country but it sits in the middle  of belgium  france and germany and it's not on the  government's list of countries to avoid  in the balkans rates are also surging  with montenegro  particularly hit hard and in spain the  rate of infection has risen  by 28.4 points that's a rise of  260 percent in the number of people  who have tested positive in the last two  weeks  one of the reasons for spain's rapid  rise in cases  is the country's northern regions rates  in aragon  are 237.9 per 100  000 people and neighboring navara and  catalonia  are also high so are these spikes a  cause  for concern here in europe we've managed  to suppress the pandemic a fair amount  in many countries  but we are seeing up surges in caseloads  in  spain in italy and and increases new  cases per day here in the uk  so there are concerns that here in  europe who may be heading towards a  second wave  it's a bit early to be sure of that yet  but these are certainly concerning  numbers that we're seeing right now  and just like in spain there are places  in the uk where the number of infections  is going up in blackburn with darwin  there has been a 98  increase in infection rates in the last  fortnight and in oldham  where some lockdown measures have been  reintroduced today the rate of infection  65.4  that's an increase of 240 percent  in the last week remember this graph  we used to see it every day at 